      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around a railroad; the Manistee and North-Eastern Railroad reached the lakes in 1889, later replaced by Pere Marquette.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interlochen%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1928, Joseph E. Maddy founded the National High School Orchestra Camp, the seed of what is now Interlochen Center for the Arts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interlochen%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Conservationists like Roosevelt and Muir inspired local efforts that turned timber town into a tourism hub, preserving pine forests for future generations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.interlochen.org/stories/timber-to-tourism-how-conservation-shaped-interlochen-michigan",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
